cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this software, please cite it as below."
title: "appfl-bio-suite: cross-silo federated learning experiments in computational biology"
abstract: >-
  A downstream extension package for APPFL that packages federated learning
  experiments in computational biology -- a FLamby Fed-Heart-Disease benchmark, a
  federated GWAS with polygenic-score evaluation, and federated cross-ancestry
  fine-mapping governed by the GA4GH DUO, DRS, TRS and TES specifications -- together
  with the operational tooling, partner-onboarding workflow, federation network map,
  and reproducible data-simulation pipeline needed to run them across independently
  administered HPC centers over Globus Compute.
type: software
license: MIT
version: 0.2.0

# TODO before the first public release: add ORCID and affiliation for each author, and
# add any co-authors from the partner sites who should be credited.
authors:
  - family-names: "Joel"
    given-names: "Matthew"

keywords:
  - federated learning
  - genome-wide association study
  - statistical fine-mapping
  - GA4GH
  - polygenic risk score
  - differential privacy
  - Globus Compute
  - APPFL
  - reproducible research

references:
  - type: article
    title: >-
      Simulation Benchmarking of Differential Privacy and Federated Learning for
      Secure Genetic Analysis and Polygenic Risk Scoring
    authors:
      - family-names: "Joel"
        given-names: "Matthew"
    notes: >-
      The GWAS experiment reproduces this paper's simulation design. See
      docs/experiments/gwas/ABOUT.md, and read docs/experiments/gwas/DATA.md for the
      documented limits on reproducing the published figures from this repository alone.

  - type: software
    title: "APPFL: An open-source package for privacy-preserving federated learning"
    repository-code: "https://github.com/APPFL/APPFL"
    license: MIT

  - type: article
    title: >-
      FLamby: Datasets and Benchmarks for Cross-Silo Federated Learning in Realistic
      Healthcare Settings
    year: 2022
    notes: "NeurIPS 2022 Datasets and Benchmarks track. https://arxiv.org/abs/2210.04620"
